n\n^{1}7708 Hackamore Drive, Potomac MD 20854, Montgomery Blair High School\n\n  * Published Online: 04/12/2025\n\n  * DOI: https://doi.org/10.61091/jcmcc128-19\n  * Download PDF\n  * Cite\n\n  * Copyright Link\n  * License\n\n#### Abstract\n\nThis paper provides new lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers using Rabung’s method, which colors based on the discrete logarithm modulo some prime. Through a distributed computing project with 500 volunteers over one year, we checked all primes up to 950 million, compared to 27 million in previous work. We point to evidence that the van der Waerden number for r colors and progression length k is roughly {r}^{k}.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBen Green: New lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers (NTWS 069) - YouTube (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LgFotGEvql8)\nciteturn9youtube18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; # Ben Green: New lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers (NTWS 069) ... I will give a general overview of this kind of problem (which can be formulated in terms of finding lower bounds for so-called van der Waerden numbers), and an overview of the construction and some of the ingredients which enter into the proof.\n# Ben Green: New lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers (NTWS 069)\nBy: Number Theory Web Seminar (1620 followers)\nPublished: 2023-08-06, 419 views\n\n## Description\nAbstract: Colour {1,..,N} red and blue, in such a manner that no 3 of the blue elements are in arithmetic progression. How long an arithmetic progression of red elements must there be? It had been speculated based on numerical evidence that there must always be a red progression of length about sqrt{N}. I will describe a construction which shows that this is not the case - in fact, there is a colouring with no red progression of length more than about exp ((log N)^{3/4}), and in particular less than any fixed power of N.\nI will give a general overview of this kind of problem (which can be formulated in terms of finding lower bounds for so-called van der Waerden numbers), and an overview of the construction and some of the ingredients which enter into the proof. The collection of techniques brought to bear on the problem is quite extensive and includes tools from diophantine approximation, additive number theory and, at one point, random matrix theory.  \n\nLink to slides: URL...\n\nNumber Theory Web Seminar: URL\n\nOriginal air date: \nThursday, February 25, 2021 (8am PST, 11am EST, 4pm GMT, 5pm CET, 6pm Israel Standard Time, 9:30pm Indian Standard Time)\nFriday, February 26, 2021 (12am CST, 3am AEDT, 5am NZDT)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJ. COMBIN. MATH. COMBIN. COMPUT. 128 (2026) 305–315 (https://combinatorialpress.com/article/jcmcc/Volume%20128/new-lower-bounds-for-van-der-waerden-numbers-using-distributed-computing.pdf)\nciteturn9search19 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; # New lower bounds for Van der Waerden numbers using distributed computing ... This paper provides new lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers using Rabung’s method, which colors based on the discrete logarithm modulo some prime.\nJ. COMBIN. MATH. COMBIN. COMPUT. 128 (2026) 305–315\n\nJournal of Combinatorial Mathematics and Combinatorial Computing  \nwww.combinatorialpress.com/jcmcc\n\nCombinatorial Press\n\n# New lower bounds for Van der Waerden numbers using distributed computing\n\nDaniel Monroe*\n\n## ABSTRACT\n\nThis paper provides new lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers using Rabung’s method, which colors based on the discrete logarithm modulo some prime. Through a distributed computing project with 500 volunteers over one year, we checked all primes up to 950 million, compared to 27 million in previous work. We point to evidence that the van der Waerden number for r colors and progression length k is roughly r^k.\n\n*Keywords:* van der Waerden numbers, discrete logarithm coloring, arithmetic progression  \n*2020 Mathematics Subject Classification:* 11B25, 05D10, 11Y35.\n\n# 1. Introduction\n\nVan der Waerden’s Theorem [22] states that for any k and r there is an n for which any r-coloring of [n] admits a monochromatic arithmetic progression of length k, where [n] is taken to denote {1, 2, . . . , n}. The van der Waerden number W(k, r) is the smallest such n.\n\nOnly seven nontrivial van der Waerden numbers are known exactly. These are the entries in Table 1 without a > symbol. For other k and r, only upper and lower bounds are available.\n\nVan der Waerden’s original proof gave bounds that were not primitive recursive. Shelah [20] gave a proof of bounds that were primitive recursive; in particular, he showed that W(k, r) lies no higher than the fifth class of the Grzegorczyk hierarchy.\n\n* Corresponding author.  \nPublished 04 Dec 2025.  \nDOI: 10.61091/jcmcc128-19  \n© 2025 The Author(s). Published by Combinatorial Press. This is an open access article under the CC BY license (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmall van der Waerden numbers: current bounds and history · Cairn Commons (https://cairn-commons.com/problems/van-der-waerden-numbers)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday;   * Wikipedia — Van der Waerden number\n  * Monroe, New lower bounds for van der Waerden numbers using distributed computing (arXiv 1603.03301)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://jiehan773.github.io/)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; #### Preprints on ArXiv ... Jie Han, Hongliang Lu, Bin Wang, and Feihong Yuan. ... Exact Minimum d-Degree Thresholds for Hypergraph Perfect Matchings.\n  6.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScientific | mathtube.org (https://www.mathtube.org/category/class/scientific?page=72)\nciteturn9search5